## Claim
Text: "Donald Trump said "Last month, I took on Big Pharma. You think that is easy? I signed orders that would massively lower the cost of your prescription drugs.""
Claim author: Donald Trump
Claim date: August 27, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### 1. Did Donald Trump, between July 1, 2020, and August 26, 2020, sign any executive orders specifically aimed at reducing prescription drug prices?
Answer: Yes, Donald Trump signed multiple executive orders specifically aimed at reducing prescription drug prices during the specified timeframe, including four on July 24, 2020, that addressed insulin and epinephrine costs, drug importation, rebate reforms, and international pricing benchmarks.

Source URL: https://www.ebglaw.com/insights/publications/president-trump-signs-executive-orders-on-drug-pricing-and-domestic-supply-chain-reform

### 2. What were the titles and official numbers of any executive orders related to prescription drug costs signed by Donald Trump in the timeframe of "last month" before August 27, 2020?
Answer: According to the source, on July 24, 2020, President Trump signed three executive orders: the "Executive Order on Access to Affordable Life-saving Medications," the "Executive Order on Increasing Drug Importation to Lower Prices for American Patients," and the "Executive Order on Lowering Prices for Patients by Eliminating Kickbacks to Middlemen."

Source URL: https://www.whitecase.com/insight-alert/uncertainty-drug-pricing-remains-following-trump-administration-executive-orders

### 3. What were the primary mechanisms (e.g., linking to international prices, reforming rebates, enabling importation) specified in the executive orders signed by Donald Trump to lower prescription drug costs?
Answer: The primary mechanisms specified in the executive orders were: enabling access to 340B program prices for insulin and epinephrine for certain low-income individuals; eliminating anti-kickback safe harbors for manufacturer rebates to middlemen in Medicare Part D to ensure discounts are passed to patients; opening pathways for the importation of prescription drugs from other countries like Canada; and proposing a "most favored nations clause" to tie Medicare prices to those paid in other countries.

Source URL: https://www.whitecase.com/insight-alert/uncertainty-drug-pricing-remains-following-trump-administration-executive-orders

### 4. Were the executive orders signed by Donald Trump to lower drug costs legally binding directives or were they primarily symbolic and requiring further action from federal agencies?
Answer: The executive orders signed by Donald Trump were not immediately legally binding directives but were primarily subject to regulatory review and further action, as the source notes they were "subject to the regulatory review process" and "may take months for the administration to finalize," with Trump even delaying one order to allow industry negotiation.

