### 7. Did pharmaceutical industry groups or companies publicly describe Donald Trump's actions as "taking them on" in a challenging manner?
Answer: Yes, pharmaceutical industry groups publicly opposed President Trump's executive orders in strong terms that framed his actions as a confrontational challenge. The industry trade group PhRMA and other executives described the orders as a "reckless distraction" and a "radical and dangerous policy," indicating they perceived his moves as a direct and aggressive challenge to the industry.

Source URL: https://www.cnbc.com/2020/07/24/trump-announces-series-of-executive-orders-aimed-at-lowering-drug-costs.html

### 8. Prior to August 2020, what specific actions, if any, had the Trump administration already taken regarding prescription drug pricing that these orders built upon or were distinct from?
Answer: Prior to the July 2020 executive orders, the Trump administration had taken several earlier actions on prescription drug pricing: it released the 2018 "Blueprint to Lower Drug Prices and Reduce Out-of-Pocket Costs," which served as the foundation for the later orders; in February 2019, it initiated a federal rulemaking process to eliminate certain prescription drug rebates from Anti-Kickback Statute safe harbor protection (though this was withdrawn in July 2019 due to projected increases in beneficiary premiums and federal spending); in December 2019, it issued a proposed rule and draft guidance to authorize importation of certain prescription drugs from Canada; and in 2018, CMS released an advanced notice of proposed rulemaking for the International Pricing Index (IPI) model for Medicare Part B drugs, though a proposed rule remained under review at OMB as of mid-2019. Additionally, in May 2020, CMS introduced the Part D Senior Savings Model to reduce insulin out-of-pocket costs for Medicare beneficiaries. As the source notes, the July 2020 executive orders "largely attempt to resurrect drug pricing reform initiatives originally mentioned in the Trump administration's 2018 'Blueprint to Lower Drug Prices and Reduce Out-of-Pocket Costs.'"

