## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, Donald Trump did sign multiple executive orders on July 24, 2020—within "last month" before his August 27, 2020 statement—that were explicitly aimed at reducing prescription drug prices, such as by enabling drug importation and tying Medicare prices to international rates. However, these orders were not immediately legally binding; they were subject to regulatory review and further agency action, with implementation delayed and not completed during his presidency. There is no evidence from non-partisan sources that these orders resulted in a massive reduction in out-of-pocket costs for consumers, and while pharmaceutical groups opposed the orders, suggesting a confrontational approach, the actual impact on lowering costs remains unproven.

The key missing information is concrete evidence that the executive orders actually led to a massive reduction in prescription drug costs during Trump's term, which would be necessary to fully validate the claim's assertion about effectiveness. Given the records show no implementation or documented cost reduction, I can proceed to a conclusion.

In conclusion, while the fact-check confirms that Donald Trump signed executive orders targeting prescription drug prices and faced industry opposition, the claim that these orders "would massively lower the cost" is not supported by the evidence, as the orders were not implemented during his presidency and there is no proof of massive cost reduction. Therefore, the claim is refuted. `refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
While Donald Trump did sign multiple executive orders on July 24, 2020, aimed at lowering prescription drug prices—including measures on [importation](https://www.whitecase.com/insight-alert/uncertainty-drug-pricing-remains-following-trump-administration-executive-orders) and [tying Medicare prices to international rates](https://www.whitecase.com/insight-alert/uncertainty-drug-pricing-remains-following-trump-administration-executive-orders)—these orders were not immediately legally binding and were subject to [regulatory review](https://www.cnbc.com/2020/07/24/trump-announces-series-of-executive-orders-aimed-at-lowering-drug-costs.html). They were not implemented during his presidency, and there is [no evidence from non-partisan sources](https://nypost.com/2020/07/25/trump-takes-victory-lap-after-prescription-drug-executive-order/) that they resulted in a massive reduction in out-of-pocket costs for consumers. Although industry groups [publicly opposed the orders](https://www.cnbc.com/2020/07/24/trump-announces-series-of-executive-orders-aimed-at-lowering-drug-costs.html), the claim of having "massively lower[ed] the cost" remains unproven due to the lack of implementation and documented impact.